    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Changing Directory"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "As we know, Python thinks the current directory is the directory containing our Python file."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If we need to change the current working directory, we can use the chdir() method of the os module."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "import os\n\n# print current working directory\nprint('Before CWD =',os.getcwd())\n\n# change current working directory\nos.chdir('D:/Projects')\n\n# print current working directory\nprint('After CWD =', os.getcwd())"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Sample Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "content": "Before CWD = c:\\Users\\lenovo\\Desktop\\Files\nAfter CWD = D:\\Projects"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Before the current working directory was c:\\Users\\lenovo\\Desktop\\Files."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Then, we changed the current working directory to D:/Projects using this code:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "content": "os.chdir('D:/Projects')"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Now, if we again print the current working directory, we get this new path."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "By the way, chdir means change directory."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
